-- In the first policy year the paragraph does not apply, and nothing else in 9.3 or 9.4
-- says what follows when premiums ARE paid but the surrender value cannot meet a monthly
-- deduction (90% of the first year's premium goes to the initial charge). That case is
-- declined (NOTES.md, findings).
@ref Article 9.4, first and second paragraphs (src 353-367)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 9.4 does not say what follows when the surrender value cannot meet a monthly deduction in the first policy year` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 9.4 does not say what follows when the surrender value cannot meet a monthly deduction in the first policy year"

@ref Article 9.4, second paragraph (src 364-367)
GIVEN `the date`           IS A DATE
      `the effective date` IS A DATE
      `the account`        IS A `The account at a monthly deduction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 9.4 — a grace period begins at a monthly deduction on` `the date` `, for a contract effective on` `the effective date` `, given` `the account` MEANS
    IF   NOT `the shortfall`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE IF   `the policy year in which` `the date` `falls, for a contract effective on` `the effective date` AT LEAST 2
         THEN TRUE
         ELSE `Article 9.4 does not say what follows when the surrender value cannot meet a monthly deduction in the first policy year`
    WHERE
        `the shortfall` MEANS
            `the account`'s `surrender value` LESS THAN `the account`'s `risk premium` PLUS `the account`'s `policy fee`

-- The contract stays in force for 60 days from the day the surrender value was "most
-- recently" found not enough. After them it ends automatically, with no surrender value,
-- unless the Company has received a payment it calculates as enough. Read with the 60 days
-- counted from the determination that opened the grace period (NOTES.md fork register);
-- the literal "most recently" restarts the count at every later monthly determination,
-- and the findings module shows what that does.
